export const docsContent: Record<string, string> = {
  "getting-started/quickstart": "---\ntitle: \"Quick Start Guide\"\ndescription: \"Get started with Click Vault in under 3 minutes. Zero-knowledge secret management locked behind your fingerprint.\"\ncategory: \"Getting Started\"\norder: 1\n---\n\n# Quick Start Guide\n\nClick Vault provides zero-knowledge secret management for developers, teams, and autonomous AI agents. All secrets are encrypted and decrypted directly on your hardware using biometric **Passkeys** (Apple Touch ID, Face ID, Windows Hello, or YubiKeys).\n\nLocal secrets are managed through our rich web vault interface at [`https://vault.vercel.click`](https://vault.vercel.click), which securely connects to a lightweight local service daemon running on your computer.\n\nPlaintext credentials **never leave your device** and are injected directly into your application's memory at runtime.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Install the CLI\n\nInstall the official Click CLI via Homebrew (macOS & Linux) or using our standalone installer script:\n\n```bash\n# macOS & Linux via Homebrew\nbrew install vercelclick/tap/click-cli\n\n# Or standalone curl installer\ncurl -LsSf https://github.com/vercelclick/cli/releases/latest/download/click-cli-installer.sh | sh\n```\n\nVerify your installation:\n\n```bash\nclick --version\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Start the Local Vault Service\n\nClick uses a lightweight background service to bridge between your local `.click/` project directory and the web vault UI at [`https://vault.vercel.click`](https://vault.vercel.click):\n\n```bash\n# Start the local vault daemon service\nclick service start\n```\n\n> **Using macOS?** Safari and other browsers may block secure HTTPS web pages from communicating with local services by default. Run this one-time command to install and trust the local HTTPS certificate:\n> ```bash\n> click service install-certificates\n> ```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Attach Your Project to the Web Vault\n\nNavigate to your application repository and attach it:\n\n```bash\ncd my-project\nclick attach\n```\n\nRunning `click attach` will:\n1. Initialize the local `.click/` directory in your project.\n2. Automatically open [`https://vault.vercel.click`](https://vault.vercel.click) in your default browser.\n3. Establish a secure local HTTPS link between the web vault and your local service.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Manage Secrets in the Web Vault\n\nIn the web interface at [`https://vault.vercel.click`](https://vault.vercel.click):\n\n1. **Register Your Passkey:** Authenticate via Touch ID, Face ID, or your hardware security key. Your private key stays safely inside your device's hardware Secure Enclave.\n2. **Add Your Secrets:** Create development, staging, or production credentials (e.g. `DATABASE_URL`, `STRIPE_SECRET_KEY`).\n3. **Automatic Client-Side Encryption:** When you hit save, the web vault encrypts secrets **directly inside your browser** using recipient public keys and writes the encrypted ciphertext files into your local `.click/secrets/` directory.\n\n**Click servers never see or hold the keys to your plaintext secrets.**\n\n---\n\n## 5. Run Your Application with Just-In-Time Injection\n\nYou do **not** need to create `.env` files or install any third-party SDKs in your code. Simply prepend `click run --` to your standard start command:\n\n```bash\n# Node.js / Next.js\nclick run -- npm run dev\n\n# Python\nclick run -- python main.py\n\n# Go\nclick run -- go run main.go\n\n# Rust\nclick run -- cargo run\n```\n\n### What Happens:\n1. Click prompts your hardware Passkey (e.g., Touch ID).\n2. Secrets are decrypted in **sub-500ms** strictly inside local RAM.\n3. The child process (`npm run dev`) is spawned with decrypted environment variables.\n4. When the process terminates, all secrets evaporate from memory. **Zero `.env` files written to disk.**\n\n---\n\n## 6. Access Secrets in Your Code\n\nYour application code remains 100% standard and vendor-neutral:\n\n```typescript\n// Standard TypeScript / JavaScript\nconst databaseUrl = process.env.DATABASE_URL;\nconst stripeKey = process.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY;\n```\n\n```python\n# Standard Python\nimport os\ndatabase_url = os.getenv(\"DATABASE_URL\")\nstripe_key = os.getenv(\"STRIPE_SECRET_KEY\")\n```\n\n---\n\n## 7. Exporting Secrets (Optional)\n\nIf you need secrets for external tools or legacy scripts, you can export them directly:\n\n```bash\n# Export to standard .env format\nclick export --format=dot-env > .env\n\n# Export to JSON\nclick export --format=json > secrets.json\n```\n\n---\n\n## Next Steps\n\n- [CLI Command Reference](/docs/cli/commands) - Complete reference for all CLI commands and flags.\n- [Zero-Knowledge Architecture](/docs/getting-started/architecture) - Learn how Click guarantees mathematical privacy.\n- [Local GitOps Storage](/docs/storage/gitops-local) - Understand how `.click/secrets/` files are structured and version-controlled.\n- [CI/CD & GitHub Actions](/docs/automation/ci-cd) - Automate deployments with scoped machine keys.\n",
  "getting-started/architecture": "---\ntitle: \"Zero-Knowledge Architecture\"\ndescription: \"How Click ensures your plaintext secrets never touch our servers under any condition.\"\ncategory: \"Getting Started\"\norder: 2\n---\n\n# Zero-Knowledge Architecture\n\nUnlike traditional secret managers (such as Doppler, Infisical, or HashiCorp Vault) which receive or decrypt your credentials on their cloud servers, **Click operates on a strict End-to-End Zero-Knowledge model**.\n\n---\n\n## The Core Cryptographic Guarantee\n\n```\nDeveloper Device (Touch ID / Passkey)\n    │\n    ▼ (Client-Side Encryption in RAM)\nCiphertext Blob (X25519-ChaCha20-Poly1305)\n    │\n    ▼ (Blind Sync Over TLS)\nClick Cloud Vault / Git Repo\n    ▲\n    │ (Encrypted Bytes Only - No Decryption Keys)\n    │\nTeammate Device (Touch ID / Passkey)\n    │\n    ▼ (Client-Side Decryption in RAM)\nSpawn Child Process (click run -- npm start)\n```\n\nAt **no point** does Click's server infrastructure possess:\n1. Your private encryption keys.\n2. The ability to decrypt your secrets.\n3. Access to unencrypted plaintext credentials.\n\n---\n\n## Why \"Secret Zero\" is Eliminated\n\nIn traditional secret management:\n- A cloud KMS master key, unseal key, or master service token acts as **\"Secret Zero\"**.\n- If that master key or the cloud server memory is breached, **all customer secrets are exposed**.\n\nClick completely eliminates Secret Zero:\n- There is **no master encryption key** in Click Vault.\n- Every contributor and machine has an independent asymmetric public/private keypair.\n- When an authorized user updates a secret, their device encrypts it separately for each recipient's public key.\n- Breaching Click's database yields only random-looking ciphertext strings.\n\n---\n\n## Subpoena & Breach Immunity\n\nBecause Click mathematically lacks the private keys required for decryption:\n- **Server breaches:** An attacker who gains full root access to Click's database sees only opaque encrypted blobs.\n- **Rogue insiders:** Click employees cannot view, export, or leak your credentials.\n- **Subpoenas & warrants:** Click cannot comply with requests to turn over plaintext data because we do not have the technical means to decrypt it.\n",
  "core-concepts/passkeys": "---\ntitle: \"Passkeys & Biometrics\"\ndescription: \"How WebAuthn PRF and hardware Secure Enclaves replace master passwords with fingerprint-locked cryptography.\"\ncategory: \"Core Concepts\"\norder: 3\n---\n\n# Passkeys & Hardware Security\n\nPasswords and master tokens are the #1 vector for credential theft, phishing attacks, and corporate data leaks. Click completely replaces master passwords with **Passkeys** backed by device hardware security modules.\n\n---\n\n## How It Works: WebAuthn PRF Extension\n\nClick uses the **WebAuthn PRF (Pseudo-Random Function)** extension to turn biometric authenticators into cryptographic key generators:\n\n1. **Hardware Isolation:** When you touch your fingerprint sensor (Apple Touch ID, Windows Hello, or Android Biometrics) or insert a FIDO2 YubiKey, the authenticator derives a 256-bit symmetric key inside the physical chip.\n2. **Key Protection:** The private key **never leaves the physical Secure Enclave**.\n3. **Deterministic Derivation:** Using a cryptographic salt, the exact same device and biometric sample reproduce the exact key needed to decrypt project secrets locally in RAM.\n\n---\n\n## Supported Hardware Authenticators\n\n- **Apple macOS / iOS:** Touch ID, Face ID, Apple Watch unlock via Apple Secure Enclave.\n- **Windows 11:** Windows Hello PIN, Facial Recognition, and Fingerprint via TPM 2.0.\n- **Linux:** FIDO2 USB Security Keys (YubiKey, SoloKey, Nitrokey) via PAM / libfido2.\n- **Hardware Security Keys:** YubiKey 5 Series, Feitian, Google Titan.\n\n---\n\n## Lost Device Protection\n\nIf a developer loses their laptop:\n- The private key remains locked behind the device's hardware biometric gate and TPM/Secure Enclave encryption.\n- An administrator can instantly revoke the contributor's public key in the project settings.\n- Subsequent secret updates will no longer be encrypted for that contributor, cutting off their access immediately without needing to rotate database passwords.\n",
  "core-concepts/jit-injection": "---\ntitle: \"Just-In-Time (JIT) Secret Injection\"\ndescription: \"Injecting decrypted secrets directly into process RAM to permanently eliminate unencrypted .env files.\"\ncategory: \"Core Concepts\"\norder: 4\n---\n\n# Just-In-Time (JIT) Secret Injection\n\nFor over a decade, developers have relied on unencrypted `.env` files to configure applications locally. This practice creates massive security vulnerabilities:\n- Plaintext credentials sit unencrypted on developer laptops.\n- Files sync to personal clouds (Dropbox, iCloud, OneDrive).\n- Credentials accidentally get committed to public or private Git repositories.\n- Malicious npm, pip, or cargo packages can scan for and steal `.env` files.\n\n---\n\n## The Click JIT Model\n\nWith Click, you never write unencrypted `.env` files to disk. Instead, you use the runtime injector:\n\n```bash\nclick run -- npm run dev\n```\n\n### Execution Lifecycle:\n1. **Approval:** Click triggers your biometric Passkey prompt.\n2. **Decryption in RAM:** Secrets are fetched from local Git storage or Cloud Vault and decrypted strictly in memory.\n3. **Process Spawn:** Click spawns your command as a child process, passing decrypted secrets directly into the process's environment variables (`process.env`, `os.environ`, etc.).\n4. **Instant Evaporation:** When your app terminates, the memory is freed by the OS. Zero plaintext bytes are ever written to disk.\n\n---\n\n## Exporting for Legacy Tools\n\nIf you must integrate with a legacy Docker build or tool that strictly requires a file on disk, you can temporarily export:\n\n```bash\nclick export --format=.env > .env\n```\n\n*Note: Be sure to add `.env` to your `.gitignore` file.*\n",
  "storage/gitops-local": "---\ntitle: \"Local GitOps Storage\"\ndescription: \"Manage encrypted secrets locally through vault.vercel.click and commit them directly to Git.\"\ncategory: \"Storage Models\"\norder: 5\n---\n\n# Local GitOps Storage\n\nClick allows you to store encrypted secrets directly inside your Git repository under `.click/secrets/` while managing them through an intuitive, visual dashboard at [`https://vault.vercel.click`](https://vault.vercel.click).\n\nBecause every secret is encrypted with the public keys of authorized contributors using authenticated asymmetric encryption (X25519-ChaCha20-Poly1305), it is **completely safe to commit these files to public or private version control**.\n\n---\n\n## How It Works: Local Service + Web Vault\n\nManaging secrets in config files and terminal TTYs is error-prone. Click solves this by pairing the Web Vault with a local background daemon:\n\n```\n┌─────────────────────────────────┐\n│     https://vault.vercel.click   │  Web Vault Interface\n│     (Client-side Passkey Crypto)│\n└────────────────┬────────────────┘\n                 │ Secure Local HTTPS\n                 ▼\n┌─────────────────────────────────┐\n│     click service (localhost)   │  Local Vault Daemon\n└────────────────┬────────────────┘\n                 │ Direct File I/O\n                 ▼\n┌─────────────────────────────────┐\n│     my-project/.click/secrets/  │  Encrypted Git Repository Files\n└─────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n1. **Start the local service:** Run `click service start` (and `click service install-certificates` if on macOS Safari).\n2. **Attach your project:** Run `click attach` inside your repository directory.\n3. **Manage via Web Vault:** The browser interface at [`https://vault.vercel.click`](https://vault.vercel.click) connects to your local service. You can create environments, invite teammates, and edit secrets visually.\n4. **On-Device Encryption:** All encryption is executed locally inside your browser via your Passkey. The local service writes the resulting ciphertext into `.click/secrets/`.\n5. **Git Commit:** Safely commit `.click/secrets/` to Git. Teammates pull the repository and run `click run` to decrypt in memory.\n\n---\n\n## Directory Structure\n\nA project configured for local storage has the following structure:\n\n```\nmy-project/\n├── .click/\n│   ├── config.json               # Project metadata & environments\n│   ├── contributors.json         # Public keys of team members\n│   ├── access_keys.json          # Machine access keys\n│   └── secrets/\n│       ├── development/\n│       │   ├── DATABASE_URL.json\n│       │   └── API_KEY.json\n│       └── production/\n│           ├── DATABASE_URL.json\n│           └── STRIPE_KEY.json\n├── package.json\n└── src/\n```\n\n---\n\n## Secret File Format\n\nInside `.click/secrets/production/DATABASE_URL.json`:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"definition\": {\n    \"name\": \"DATABASE_URL\",\n    \"env_name\": \"production\"\n  },\n  \"values\": [\n    {\n      \"target\": \"user_bill_public_key_id\",\n      \"encrypted_value\": \"m-a1vXJNu0TXSXyf_tfEsvtiWGHPXks...\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"target\": \"ci_github_actions_machine_key_id\",\n      \"encrypted_value\": \"uXj6yPZTyuGIf2Jqhh4G9IQuWLDA...\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nNotice that each secret file holds multiple encrypted payloads — one for each authorized recipient! Only the owner of the matching private key can decrypt their respective payload.\n\n---\n\n## Benefits of Local GitOps Storage\n\n- **Visual Management via Web Vault:** Enjoy a full-featured dashboard at [`https://vault.vercel.click`](https://vault.vercel.click) without giving up local data ownership.\n- **100% Offline-First Execution:** Run and test on airplanes or trains with zero internet connectivity.\n- **Atomic Branching & Merging:** Secrets branch, merge, and diff alongside application code.\n- **Zero Cloud Infrastructure:** Free for open source projects, solo builders, and air-gapped teams.\n",
  "storage/cloud-vault": "---\ntitle: \"Cloud Vault Synchronization\"\ndescription: \"Hosted zero-knowledge team synchronization, granular role-based access control, and audit logs.\"\ncategory: \"Storage Models\"\norder: 6\n---\n\n# Cloud Vault Synchronization\n\nFor teams and enterprises that need instant secret synchronization without managing Git commits, Click offers a hosted **Cloud Vault** service at `https://vault.vercel.click`.\n\n---\n\n## Zero-Knowledge Guarantee on Cloud\n\nThe Cloud Vault operates as a blind ciphertext storage and relay engine:\n- When a teammate modifies a secret in the web UI or CLI, encryption occurs **locally on their machine**.\n- Only the encrypted ciphertext is transmitted to the Click Cloud Vault.\n- Click's database and servers hold **zero decryption keys**.\n\n---\n\n## Features\n\n- **Granular RBAC:** Define Admin, Contributor, and Read-Only roles per project and per environment.\n- **Multi-Environment Isolation:** Segregate Development, Staging, and Production secrets.\n- **Point-in-Time History & Rollback:** Review who modified a secret and revert to previous versions with a single click.\n- **Team Workspaces:** Group multiple projects, microservices, and developers under unified organizational workspaces.\n",
  "automation/ci-cd": "---\ntitle: \"CI/CD & GitHub Actions\"\ndescription: \"Programmatic access for automated build runners with automatic console log masking.\"\ncategory: \"Automation & AI\"\norder: 7\n---\n\n# CI/CD & Automated Pipelines\n\nAutomated continuous integration runners (such as GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, CircleCI, and Docker builds) do not have human fingerprints. For these headless environments, Click provisions **Machine Access Keys**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Generate a Machine Access Key\n\nOn the vault.vercel.click UI, select the desire project(local or cloud); Choose Grant access and select the necessary environment(s) (e.g., `staging` or `production`). Authorize the key generation with your finger print.\n\nStore this key as an encrypted secret in your repository settings (e.g., `CLICK_ACCESS_KEY` in GitHub Secrets).\n\n---\n\n## 2. GitHub Actions Integration\n\nUse Click CLI directly inside your GitHub Actions workflow:\n\n```yaml\nname: Test and Build\non: [push]\n\njobs:\n  build:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n\n      - name: Install Click CLI\n        run: |\n          curl -LsSf https://github.com/vercelclick/cli/releases/latest/download/click-cli-installer.sh | sh\n\n      - name: Run Tests with Scoped Secrets\n        run: |\n          click run --access-key=\"${{ secrets.CLICK_ACCESS_KEY }}\" --env=staging -- npm test\n```\n\n---\n\n## Automatic Secret Masking\n\nClick CLI automatically hooks stdout and stderr streams in CI environments. Any decrypted secret values printed by your build scripts or test runners are **automatically masked with `***`** to prevent accidental credential leakage in public or private workflow logs.\n",
  "cli/commands": "---\ntitle: \"CLI Command Reference\"\ndescription: \"Complete command-line interface documentation for the Click Vault CLI tool.\"\ncategory: \"CLI Reference\"\norder: 8\n---\n\n# CLI Command Reference\n\nThe `click` command-line interface is a zero-knowledge secure vault utility where all encryption and decryption operations occur on-device via Passkeys and hardware security modules.\n\n```bash\nclick <COMMAND>\n```\n\n---\n\n## Command Overview\n\n| Command | Description |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| [`click run`](#click-run) | Run a command with decrypted secrets injected into environment variables |\n| [`click export`](#click-export) | Export decrypted secrets to a file or pipe stdout |\n| [`click attach`](#click-attach) | Attach a directory as a local project to be managed via `vault.vercel.click` |\n| [`click service`](#click-service) | Manage the local vault daemon service |\n\n---\n\n## `click run`\n\nRuns a command with decrypted secrets injected directly into its environment variables. Secrets are decrypted in volatile RAM on-the-fly via your Passkey (or machine access key) and are automatically masked in GitHub Actions console logs.\n\n```bash\nclick run [OPTIONS] [COMMAND]...\n```\n\n### Arguments\n\n- `<COMMAND>` — The command to execute with injected secrets. Supports full standard `sh` syntax.\n\n### Options\n\n| Option | Default | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `--mode <auto\\|local\\|cloud>` | `auto` | Specify whether to use local or cloud vault. Automatically inferred by the presence of a `.click/` project in the directory. |\n| `--env <ENV>` | — | The vault environment to pull secrets from (e.g. `development`, `staging`, `production`). Required when using `--access-key`. |\n| `--access-key <KEY>` | — | Scoped machine access key for automated CI/CD or headless environments. If omitted, you are prompted for biometric Passkey authorization. |\n| `--project-dir <DIR>` | `cwd` | Path to the local vault project directory. |\n| `--endpoint <URL>` | `https://api.vercel.click` | Custom API endpoint when connecting to an on-premises or private cloud deployment. |\n\n### Examples\n\n```bash\n# Human development: Authenticate via Touch ID / Passkey and run dev server\nclick run -- npm run dev\n\n# Explicitly target staging environment\nclick run --env staging -- python main.py\n\n# Automated CI runner using machine access key\nclick run --access-key=\"$CLICK_ACCESS_KEY\" --env production -- cargo run\n```\n\n---\n\n## `click export`\n\nExports decrypted secrets to standard output or a file. Secrets are automatically masked on GitHub Actions.\n\n```bash\nclick export [OPTIONS]\n```\n\n### Options\n\n| Option | Default | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `--format <FORMAT>` | `dot-env` | Output format. Supported values: `dot-env` (standard `.env`), `json`, `gh-actions` (for appending to `$GITHUB_ENV`). |\n| `--mode <auto\\|local\\|cloud>` | `auto` | Specify local or cloud vault. Inferred automatically by local directory configuration. |\n| `--env <ENV>` | — | Target vault environment (required when using `--access-key`). |\n| `--access-key <KEY>` | — | Scoped machine access key for decryption. |\n| `--project-dir <DIR>` | `cwd` | Local project directory path. |\n| `--endpoint <URL>` | `https://api.vercel.click` | Custom cloud endpoint URL. |\n\n### Examples\n\n```bash\n# Export secrets to a standard local .env file\nclick export --format=dot-env > .env\n\n# Export secrets formatted as JSON\nclick export --format=json > secrets.json\n\n# Append masked secrets to GitHub Actions environment\nclick export --access-key=\"$CLICK_ACCESS_KEY\" --env=staging --format=gh-actions >> $GITHUB_ENV\n```\n\n---\n\n## `click attach`\n\nAttaches a directory as a Click Vault local project. Once attached, you can visually manage environments, add secrets, and invite teammates through the web interface at [`https://vault.vercel.click`](https://vault.vercel.click).\n\n```bash\nclick attach [OPTIONS]\n```\n\n### Options\n\n| Option | Default | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `--target <TARGET>` | `cwd` | The directory path to attach as a local project. |\n| `--silent` | `false` | Prevent the CLI from automatically opening your default web browser after attaching. |\n\n### Examples\n\n```bash\n# Attach current directory and open browser to configure project\nclick attach\n\n# Attach specific directory without opening browser\nclick attach --target ./services/api --silent\n```\n\n---\n\n## `click service`\n\nManages the local Click Vault background service daemon. The local service runs on your machine to bridge between your local `.click/` repository files and the web vault at [`https://vault.vercel.click`](https://vault.vercel.click).\n\n```bash\nclick service <COMMAND>\n```\n\n### Subcommands\n\n| Subcommand | Description |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| `start` | Start the local vault daemon service |\n| `stop` | Stop the local vault daemon service |\n| `status` | Check if the local vault daemon is running |\n| `install-certificates` | Install local trusted HTTPS certificates for macOS Safari |\n\n---\n\n### `click service start`\n\nStarts the local service that enables [`https://vault.vercel.click`](https://vault.vercel.click) to communicate with your local machine.\n\n```bash\nclick service start [OPTIONS]\n```\n\n#### Options:\n- `--foreground` *(default: `false`)* — Runs the service as a standard interactive terminal process instead of backgrounding it.\n\n```bash\n# Start background daemon\nclick service start\n\n# Run in foreground for debugging\nclick service start --foreground\n```\n\n---\n\n### `click service stop`\n\nStops the running local background service daemon.\n\n```bash\nclick service stop\n```\n\n---\n\n### `click service status`\n\nDisplays the current operational status of the local Click service.\n\n```bash\nclick service status\n```\n\n---\n\n### `click service install-certificates`\n\nInstalls and trusts local HTTPS certificates on your machine.\n\n> **Why this is needed:** By default, macOS Safari blocks web applications running on secure HTTPS origins (like `https://vault.vercel.click`) from communicating with local services (`localhost`). Running this command installs a trusted local SSL certificate so Safari can securely interface with your local service.\n\n```bash\nclick service install-certificates\n```\n"
};
